{0 Testing a worker}

Test your worker with [workerd test] to run the compiled WebAssembly in the
Workers runtime. This checks the routes and bindings in your test setup without
deploying the worker. See {{!page-limits}Limits} for Cloudflare platform limits.

{1 Run a worker test}

+ Install [workerd] on [PATH]. The repository CI uses [npm install -g workerd].
+ Build the worker: [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js --out _worker-test].
+ Write a [workerd] config. List the files of [_worker-test] as modules
  ([.js] as [esModule], [.wasm] as [wasm]). Bind the worker to a test worker as
  a service.
+ In the test worker, call the service with [env.WORKER.fetch(url)] and compare
  the status, headers, and body. Call
  [env.WORKER.scheduled({ cron, scheduledTime })] to test the cron handler.
  To invoke the queue handler with [env.WORKER.queue(name, messages)], enable
  [service_binding_extra_handlers] on the test worker and run [workerd test
  --experimental config.capnp].
+ Run [workerd test config.capnp] for fetch and scheduled tests.

[workerd] cannot run the email handler this way. See {{!page-email}Email}.

[test/worker/run.sh] shows a working config generator. It builds
[test/worker/main.ml], then runs the suites imported by
[test/worker/tests.mjs]. Worker routes live in [test/worker/routes_*.ml]; cases
live in [test/worker/cases/*.mjs]. Queue cases use a separate
[test/worker/tests_queue.mjs] test service with [--experimental]. Set
[globalOutbound] to a test worker to answer
{!Workero.fetch} calls without the network. The repository tests exercise
compiled Wasm, JSPI waits, and the [workerd] runtime. They use JS test services
for KV, R2, and Cache. D1 uses a wrapped adapter backed by Durable Object
SQLite. These substitutes test workero calls against the adapter interfaces.
They do not test production Cloudflare storage services. The repository also
tests startup failure in [test/worker-init/], handler exports in
[test/worker-exports/] and build/package behavior in [test/cli/].

[workerd] has no Workflows engine and no email delivery, so the Workflow and
email tests use JavaScript stand-ins in [test/worker/services/]. The Container
tests in [test/worker-containers/] start real containers. They run only where
Docker and the test images exist, and print [SKIP containers] otherwise. See
{{!page-known_issues}Support and known issues} for what these tests do not
prove.

The Durable Object suite in [test/worker/cases/do.mjs] tests synchronous SQLite
queries and writes, transaction commit and rollback (including an attempt to
call [Promise.await]), hibernatable WebSocket message and close callbacks,
attachments, tags, broadcast and automatic responses. These [workerd] tests do
not evict an object between WebSocket events, so they do not prove recovery
after hibernation. They also do not test [websocket_error] or a suspension
inside a WebSocket event callback. Test eviction and platform limits in a
deployed Worker.

An exception that escapes a handler fails a service-binding call. An HTTP
request gets status 500 instead.

{1 Test code without the worker runtime}

For code that does not wait, such as parsing with {!Workero.URL}, use the
Alcotest unit tests in [test/unit/]. They compile with [(modes js)] and run in
Node via [dune test]. They do not exercise compiled Wasm or the worker runtime.
Node's JSPI support depends on the version. Use [workerd test] for worker code
that calls {!Workero.Promise.await}.
